Local wheel-planner adaptation (2026-10-22)
Source: https://github.com/wuyi2121/SCAN-Planner
Branch: ros2-community
Pinned commit: d0b921c9b05a6d291d144d60882b2e0e88d2c0e0

Retained numerical modules: cubic B-splines, L-BFGS rebound/refinement
optimization, projected A-star guidance, and the embedded L-BFGS license.

Local modifications:
- Replace ROS parameter/logging dependencies with C++ options and diagnostics;
  suppress per-solve progress output, retaining failure diagnostics.
- Observe the caller's cancellation/deadline in optimization and A-star search.
- Replace variable-length stack arrays with vectors and release all A-star
  allocation levels; initialize its empty allocation state.
- Use a request-local, wheel-only occupancy-query backend over the existing
  floor-projected boxes and explicit support. Use the shared yaw-aware rectangle
  with the configured safety margin, matching TEB and the common validator;
  this replaces the upstream twin-cylinder embodiment for this wheel robot.
  The upstream sensor fusion, 3D sliding map, global FSM, simulator, controllers,
  and boundary dead-end recovery are not included.
- Adapt position splines to the existing differential-wheel trajectory contract
  with explicit stationary boundary turns, exact initial-state constraints,
  time stretching, and the unchanged common swept-collision/support/motion checks.

This integration is a wheel adaptation, not a reproduction of the complete
upstream quadruped navigation system. Footstep planning remains independent.

Rolling-start correction (2026-10-22): time reparameterization now rebuilds
the fixed initial velocity/acceleration control points at each new knot time
and reoptimizes, instead of stretching output times and changing the physical
initial velocity. The request has no measured acceleration boundary, so the
adapter chooses bounded forward acceleration toward the allowed speed rather
than imposing zero tangential acceleration at every rolling restart. Shared
motion limits, collision/support checks, controller and admission are unchanged.

Terminal-reference correction (2026-10-22): knot timing uses the actual mean
reference interval after densification. Recompute the unobserved tangential
acceleration from that interval and the new knot time on each retiming attempt.
This prevents short references from being timed as full-spacing references,
developing reversals and amplifying their fixed boundary controls on retiming.
After stationary alignment, fix the fourth initial control point on the start
tangent as well: at zero linear velocity the first three controls alone cannot
enforce zero initial heading rate. Moving starts retain three fixed controls.
